This was nothing special, except that it is my first ascention since
I returned to play Nethack after some years of abstinence. I justed to
play Monks and Samurai and even had a semi-ascandible Tourist, but
then Saga the Samurai ate a fortune cookie which read: "They say that
a clever wizard can have stats:  18/** 24 18 24 24 24." I wished I was
a magician, so I tried a couple of Wizards. This one only got these
stats: St:18/** Dx:23 Co:18 In:23 Wi:23 Ch:17 and I don't know how to
get dexterity, intelligence & wisdom *savely* to be 24. (Please spoil
me!)
Actually, I wanted Cain to be a atheist Wizard, but two altars in the first level
and another one in the second level convinced him to return to the path
of faith. He was granted an early wish and got an SDSM, got Magicbane
form Anhur on another altar on dungeon level 5, without seeing any
clover and kind of quickly wrote a spellbook of identify with the
magic marker he started with. Sokoban had the "oR, therefore Cain
didn't bother to do the last sokoban level, he already had his shiny
dragon suite. Level 19 had a "disgusting nest", which was my first one
ever, but thank's to Magicbane & Elbereth & Cain's magic missles this
was no big threat. One shop at Minetown was a library (rare book
store), which was somehow helpful, though I forgot which book had been
there ... anyway, there are *many* nearly-useless spells in this game,
and some really powerful ones ...

The rest was fairly standard, purple worms are funny pets, the castle
had a 0:3 /WoW and a blessed tinning kid (found in the mines) is
really helpful to recycle the corpses of e.g. Giants, especially of
dead-formerly-undead giants. Is this a bug, btw? Eating these mummy or
zomby corpses should make you nauseated, but this is not the case when
the tins are blessed. Anyway, this was helpful to get the 18/**
strength (an expression which I do not fully understand, could someone
tell something about the strength values 18/01-18/99, 18/** & 25?),
and Cain had a fair appetite since he never ever found a =oSD, not one
till the very end.

"You are standing at the gate to Gehennom. Unspeakable stuffiness and
boredom lurk down there. Are you sure you want to enter?" -- "Well, I
have to, don't I?" -- "So be it." No actually Gehennom isn't to boring
with a spell of magic mapping and lots of wands of digging, but it's
still more endurable then enjoyable. It's not really hellish, since
it's not too hard for a wizard at level 17, nor are the normal
monsters there hard for anyone with AC<-10, it's more like a stupid
routine. Now don't tell me that's like Hell might actual be, I
strongly believe there is a difference between the life here & the
eternal	punishment down in the pit.
Maybe Gehennom should be made shorter, about 4-7 levels and way
harder. I don't know how to do a hard level for an high-level wizard
with Magicbane and lotsoff spells that is still possible for an
weaponless monk or a healer, but I think the DevTeam should do
something about this, and jes I know this was discuessed before --
that just doesn't make the complain in anyway dispensable.

Another thing I forgot to mention is that Anhur was somehow displeased
when Cain created 7 potions of unholy water on a neutral altar, and
there seemed to be no way of mollifing Him (even cross-aligned
unicorns were inadequade), so Cain alreading thought about changeing the
object of his faith to Thoth; but Anhur had given him Magicbane, so he
forgave Him His infamous offense and brought the Amulet to His
Altar.
The endgame levels were fun, I justed to stirp in all the vortexes,
write ?oEA to enchant my gauntlets of dexterity, my helm of brilliance
and the rest of the stuff; on the plane the very last altar I visited
was the right one, but even the Riders ain't any problem for a Wizard
like Cain (where, btw, is War? I really miss him/her!), so:

